Well, in watching it episode by episode and just four years of Michael not having a partner

259
00:11:23.880 --> 00:11:29.980
to suddenly see a woman partner with him and joke with him and like his jokes, I did.

260
00:11:30.100 --> 00:11:30.520
I cried.

261
00:11:31.400 --> 00:11:38.620
Oh, I, you know, in preparation for meeting you both today, I rewatched the episode.

262
00:11:38.860 --> 00:11:40.860
I hadn't watched the show in a long time.

263
00:11:40.860 --> 00:11:44.720
I saved her, like, popping in the room when my daughter was on her third pass of the series.

264
00:11:45.800 --> 00:11:47.460
But it is so moving.

265
00:11:47.460 --> 00:11:49.260
I think all the characters are so moving.

266
00:11:49.380 --> 00:11:53.220
There's, I know everyone's talked about this before, but that element of the family, you

267
00:11:53.220 --> 00:11:54.340
know, it's not a workplace.

268
00:11:54.340 --> 00:11:55.580
It's actually a family.

269
00:11:56.200 --> 00:11:58.640
And that really comes through so beautifully.

270
00:11:58.980 --> 00:12:03.940
But Holly and Michael, I mean, I've always said, you know, there's a lid for every pot.

271
00:12:03.940 --> 00:12:14.040
So they finally found each other, but she has a high tolerance, I suppose, for that kind

272
00:12:14.040 --> 00:12:14.480
of guy.

273
00:12:14.900 --> 00:12:19.820
Well, we always like to ask people, Amy, how did you come to be on the show?

274
00:12:20.180 --> 00:12:21.840
And we did a little research.

275
00:12:22.000 --> 00:12:24.340
Paul Lieberstein told us that they came to you.

276
00:12:24.480 --> 00:12:25.440
They were huge fans.

277
00:12:25.440 --> 00:12:30.620
But that you had simultaneously mentioned to your agent, you might like to do our show.

278
00:12:31.000 --> 00:12:31.400
Yes.

279
00:12:31.680 --> 00:12:32.940
So what was that process?

280
00:12:33.220 --> 00:12:33.340
Yeah.

281
00:12:33.440 --> 00:12:34.440
How did it go down?

282
00:12:34.740 --> 00:12:35.940
That is true.

283
00:12:36.160 --> 00:12:41.420
And the timing was eerie because I did say that to my agent.

284
00:12:41.420 --> 00:12:43.180
It was soon after the Oscars.

285
00:12:43.180 --> 00:12:46.320
And I was like, you know, I'm doing a lot of dramas.

286
00:12:46.320 --> 00:12:51.160
And if I have any kind of cachet right now at this moment, I want to do a comedy and I

287
00:12:51.160 --> 00:12:57.440
want to do a comedy like The Office because I was never good at the four camera sitcom.

288
00:12:57.640 --> 00:12:59.360
I just felt too much pressure.

289
00:12:59.520 --> 00:13:03.680
And it wasn't like I always feel like characters weren't really rooted in reality the way The

290
00:13:03.680 --> 00:13:04.220
Office was.

291
00:13:04.440 --> 00:13:06.940
And that goes back to how I met Paul Lieberstein.

292
00:13:07.100 --> 00:13:09.940
We were both employed on The Naked Truth.

293
00:13:10.860 --> 00:13:12.260
And he was a writer.

294
00:13:12.780 --> 00:13:14.800
My agent had kind of reached out.

295
00:13:14.800 --> 00:13:17.920
And at the same time, Paul had reached out, I guess, to him.

296
00:13:18.140 --> 00:13:21.800
I don't know who got there first, but it was I really think it was like about the same

297
00:13:21.800 --> 00:13:22.120
week.

298
00:13:22.940 --> 00:13:28.360
And Paul had mentioned that he was also a fan of The Wire, as were some of the other

299
00:13:28.360 --> 00:13:28.700
writers.

300
00:13:28.900 --> 00:13:36.200
So it was kind of a culmination of all those worlds coming together.

301
00:13:36.440 --> 00:13:39.960
And did they pitch the character of Holly to you or did you like have a script to read

302
00:13:39.960 --> 00:13:40.380
or anything?

303
00:13:41.300 --> 00:13:43.020
I don't remember reading a script.

304
00:13:43.020 --> 00:13:50.700
I remember saying, hearing it was a one episode only and potential love interest, which obviously

305
00:13:50.700 --> 00:13:55.660
I knew was code for, we'll see how you do, kid, if we have you back.

306
00:13:56.020 --> 00:14:02.560
I remember also because I was a big fan of the British series, David finally gets a woman

307
00:14:02.560 --> 00:14:03.580
who likes him too.

308
00:14:03.580 --> 00:14:07.600
So it was loosely based, I think, on her for the first episode.

309
00:14:07.600 --> 00:14:10.320
And then our group took it to a different level.

310
00:14:10.460 --> 00:14:15.840
So there was no script that I was privy to, but I knew the I knew the kind of general idea

311
00:14:15.840 --> 00:14:16.240
of it.

312
00:14:16.380 --> 00:14:19.680
What was it like to walk on to the set for the first time?

313
00:14:20.580 --> 00:14:21.460
Really weird.

314
00:14:23.100 --> 00:14:24.260
It's really weird.

315
00:14:24.260 --> 00:14:31.280
It's because also then, now I'm a fan of the American version and you start to know the

316
00:14:31.280 --> 00:14:35.640
set, obviously, from the comfort of your couch or your tablet or whatever it is.

317
00:14:35.980 --> 00:14:38.340
You know, when you get there, it's like, oh, everything's smaller.

318
00:14:39.060 --> 00:14:42.120
And then there you all were so warm and welcoming.

319
00:14:42.360 --> 00:14:45.340
You know, there's always an element of being the new kid in school.

320
00:14:45.340 --> 00:14:46.960
And how's that going to go down?

321
00:14:48.500 --> 00:14:53.400
But, and also you guys at this point are on a really massive, you know, big hit show.

322
00:14:53.480 --> 00:15:02.920
And it was the most down to earth, welcoming, warm group of people, like true ensemble that

323
00:15:02.920 --> 00:15:03.440
I met.

324
00:15:03.780 --> 00:15:08.300
And even to the end, I feel like, you know, you hear all those stories of shows that become

325
00:15:08.300 --> 00:15:11.740
so successful and the bad behavior starts coming out.

326
00:15:11.900 --> 00:15:15.020
And, you know, but at least I didn't see any of that.

327
00:15:15.340 --> 00:15:17.480
And I just thought it was always about the work.

328
00:15:17.660 --> 00:15:21.400
And you guys really set such a beautiful tone there.

329
00:15:21.860 --> 00:15:24.260
Well, you said you rewatched Business Ethics.

330
00:15:24.360 --> 00:15:31.280
And one of my favorite parts is when you and Michael do the Let's Get Physical.

331
00:15:31.600 --> 00:15:32.900
Yes, that's so funny.

332
00:15:33.140 --> 00:15:37.400
I watched that with my daughter and we watched that scene like two times in a row.

333
00:15:37.580 --> 00:15:39.740
Just him reaching in, trying to hit the play button.

334
00:15:40.000 --> 00:15:44.220
And then the two of you running in with those headbands and your dance number.

335
00:15:44.220 --> 00:15:46.320
How did you guys come up with all that?

336
00:15:46.620 --> 00:15:47.040
What do we do?

337
00:15:47.200 --> 00:15:49.800
Well, the headbands, I have to give credit for wardrobe.

338
00:15:50.240 --> 00:15:53.180
I mean, that was pretty wonderful and ridiculous.

339
00:15:53.620 --> 00:15:55.120
Just that having that visual.

340
00:15:55.340 --> 00:15:58.760
The dance, honestly, that's me just following Steve.

341
00:16:00.540 --> 00:16:06.980
Anything, you know, I always, anything that I was lucky enough to be partnered, paired with Steve,

342
00:16:06.980 --> 00:16:12.160
I was smart enough to get in the sidecar of that motorcycle ride.

343
00:16:12.380 --> 00:16:14.180
Like, I'm not going to lead this here.

344
00:16:14.360 --> 00:16:15.740
He's going to have a better idea.

345
00:16:16.000 --> 00:16:23.120
So that was just me kind of, you know, literally following his choreography.

346
00:16:24.500 --> 00:16:27.440
Well, I wasn't there because my character was at art school.

347
00:16:27.600 --> 00:16:31.460
So I am super bummed that I did not get to see that done live.

348
00:16:32.180 --> 00:16:34.360
Did you guys do like tons of takes of it?

349
00:16:34.500 --> 00:16:35.400
You were there, Anne.

350
00:16:35.520 --> 00:16:36.200
Do you guys remember?

351
00:16:36.700 --> 00:16:38.340
It was so hard not to laugh.

352
00:16:38.540 --> 00:16:44.020
Like, from the minute it started, and when I was rewatching it, Oscar's face says it all.

353
00:16:44.200 --> 00:16:44.920
He's in the front.

354
00:16:44.920 --> 00:16:46.320
And I was like, I don't want to be in the front.

355
00:16:46.460 --> 00:16:47.620
Please don't put me in the front.

356
00:16:48.220 --> 00:16:50.280
Because I knew I was going to get so tickled.

357
00:16:50.640 --> 00:16:53.100
And Oscar does this thing when he's trying not to break.

358
00:16:53.340 --> 00:16:57.620
You know, we know each other's tales after all those years where he kind of does this mouth open thing.

359
00:16:58.180 --> 00:17:00.060
And that's him trying not to laugh.

360
00:17:00.300 --> 00:17:01.660
So you go back and watch.

361
00:17:01.760 --> 00:17:03.160
Oscar's like, I'm going to.

362
00:17:04.680 --> 00:17:06.100
It was so funny.

363
00:17:06.880 --> 00:17:12.400
First of all, being in that conference, you know, that's kind of the most famous part of the set, right?

364
00:17:12.480 --> 00:17:13.200
Here we go.

365
00:17:13.360 --> 00:17:14.620
And someone gets to present something.

366
00:17:14.620 --> 00:17:18.700
And it's better when, again, you're paired with someone next to you.

367
00:17:18.820 --> 00:17:23.980
But those scenes where I'm alone giving the conference, my hands are like white knuckled.

368
00:17:24.000 --> 00:17:27.140
I'm like trying to be like, you know, very casual holding my hands.

369
00:17:27.220 --> 00:17:31.980
But if you look closely, my hands are just like two iron fists of nerves.

370
00:17:32.420 --> 00:17:33.040
That's the other thing.

371
00:17:33.160 --> 00:17:38.940
Like, everyone always asks me, is the office, was that a lot of improv?

372
00:17:38.940 --> 00:17:48.940
And I said, no, it was, I remember scripty coming up to me, you know, correcting me for like of instead of at.

373
00:17:49.100 --> 00:17:50.300
Like something really tiny.

374
00:17:50.700 --> 00:17:53.900
And I tell people, and tell me if I'm wrong.

375
00:17:54.040 --> 00:17:55.920
I was like, it was heavily scripted.

376
00:17:56.000 --> 00:18:02.800
And then maybe by the third take, Steve would go off script first and then everyone would follow suit.

377
00:18:02.960 --> 00:18:04.460
That's my recollection of it.

378
00:18:05.100 --> 00:18:06.420
Would you say that's right?

379
00:18:06.980 --> 00:18:07.460
Absolutely.

380
00:18:08.000 --> 00:18:08.420
Yeah.

381
00:18:08.680 --> 00:18:16.740
And then, and in that, I just think it was, again, like, you guys are so in tune with each other.

382
00:18:16.960 --> 00:18:21.120
And no one trying to beat anyone to the punchline.

383
00:18:21.120 --> 00:18:22.920
It was just a constant setup.

384
00:18:23.140 --> 00:18:25.680
And it was, I thought it was just so perfect.

385
00:18:25.700 --> 00:18:30.960
And I think that's why people can't really tell between the scripted and when it goes to improv.

386
00:18:32.160 --> 00:18:35.600
It was just a, just so perfectly done.

387
00:18:36.420 --> 00:18:39.480
I wanted to ask you about those restaurant scenes.

388
00:18:39.980 --> 00:18:40.900
Oh, yeah.

389
00:18:43.220 --> 00:18:43.780
Cooper's.

390
00:18:43.960 --> 00:18:44.400
Cooper's.

391
00:18:44.580 --> 00:18:50.400
There's that whole runner about the chastity belt where he's trying to explain what a chastity belt is.

392
00:18:50.540 --> 00:18:50.960
Oh, my God.

393
00:18:50.960 --> 00:18:53.180
And you're like a chastity belt.

394
00:18:53.500 --> 00:19:02.100
I think like the really fun part of their relationship is that it's always a surprise when like Holly's on board with him with some joke.

395
00:19:02.100 --> 00:19:08.960
And then those moments like the chastity belt where she's so horrified and yet so forgiving.

396
00:19:09.220 --> 00:19:15.420
You know, here she is, this, you know, she's still flirty with this guy and she likes him and not sure where it's going to go.

397
00:19:15.420 --> 00:19:17.820
But she's going to give that a pass.

398
00:19:18.680 --> 00:19:22.640
You know, always with Steve, it was so hard not to laugh.

399
00:19:22.880 --> 00:19:26.440
It was also like a thrill that sets in.

400
00:19:26.960 --> 00:19:29.960
Look, I remember when I was 25, I went skydiving.

401
00:19:31.100 --> 00:19:34.080
And I did it where you're strapped to the other instructor.

402
00:19:34.420 --> 00:19:41.220
And just as the airplane door opened up and he made me look out, we were about, I guess, 13,000 feet in the air.

403
00:19:41.480 --> 00:19:47.240
And I remember the thought in my head is, OK, I'm going to die or I'm going to have the time of my life.

404
00:19:47.580 --> 00:19:48.020
Pick one.

405
00:19:48.680 --> 00:19:49.840
And I remember picking.

406
00:19:50.000 --> 00:19:50.980
I'm going to have fun.

407
00:19:50.980 --> 00:20:02.220
And as nervous as I was, you know, being like in those scenes with Steve, I remember that moment too, like, oh, just pick one.

408
00:20:02.480 --> 00:20:03.540
Why don't you have fun instead?

409
00:20:03.540 --> 00:20:09.940
You know, and certainly with him at the lead, like, we're all going to look good.

410
00:20:10.160 --> 00:20:13.760
You know, he's going to take us along for the ride, I feel.

411
00:20:13.940 --> 00:20:20.360
But it was so hard not to laugh, like painfully hard not to laugh.

412
00:20:22.080 --> 00:20:31.340
But I felt like we were dropped into another place of concentration that kind of pulled it all along, if that makes sense.

413
00:20:31.880 --> 00:20:32.400
Yeah.

414
00:20:32.540 --> 00:20:33.960
Did you know Steve before?

415
00:20:34.900 --> 00:20:35.420
Yeah.

416
00:20:35.620 --> 00:20:41.160
In a very, I had this minuscule part in Dan in Real Life that Steve starred in.

417
00:20:41.300 --> 00:20:44.540
It was this big extended family comedy.

418
00:20:44.800 --> 00:20:48.260
We all, there was a whole group of us there on location in Rhode Island.

419
00:20:48.380 --> 00:20:49.720
And so we all became friends.

420
00:20:49.720 --> 00:20:55.400
And then I think it was about, like, six months after that film is when The Office came up.

421
00:20:55.460 --> 00:20:59.520
And I believe they ran the idea by Steve of casting me.

422
00:20:59.680 --> 00:21:00.780
And I guess it helped.

423
00:21:01.080 --> 00:21:04.100
I knew him from Rhode Island for that little bit.

424
00:21:04.340 --> 00:21:08.880
Well, we always ask everyone if they have any, like, favorite memories from their time on the show,

425
00:21:09.080 --> 00:21:14.760
like any kind of behind-the-scenes moment or just something that you remember that stood out to you during your time on The Office.

426
00:21:14.760 --> 00:21:16.280
Oh, my gosh.

427
00:21:16.420 --> 00:21:17.360
There's so, so much.

428
00:21:17.480 --> 00:21:24.700
I mean, just in a general note, I would honestly, no exaggeration,

429
00:21:24.820 --> 00:21:32.840
I would go home feeling like I worked out a series of sit-ups because my stomach muscles were always in play for laughing so hard.

430
00:21:32.840 --> 00:21:35.600
And I'd just go home exhausted but happy.

431
00:21:35.740 --> 00:21:37.480
And that was a nice feeling.

432
00:21:37.700 --> 00:21:46.940
But there's, and speaking of that, like, kind of the uncontrollable laughter, there's the episode where Daryl helps me move.

433
00:21:46.940 --> 00:21:49.500
And he comes in the car ride, the truck ride.

434
00:21:49.720 --> 00:21:50.080
Yes.

435
00:21:50.300 --> 00:21:50.420
Yeah.

436
00:21:52.540 --> 00:22:03.360
Me, Steve, and Craig, like, holed up in that truck all day, kind of, probably we had a little bit of lack of oxygen.

437
00:22:03.360 --> 00:22:09.940
But we couldn't, we, we just, we were in hysterics and could barely get through the scenes.

438
00:22:11.640 --> 00:22:21.960
And it was, that was, like, one of the highlights of my time there was just spending the day with those two guys, just laughing.

439
00:22:21.960 --> 00:22:28.040
I mean, I say that, but I'd also be on set and laughing all the time, too.

440
00:22:28.760 --> 00:22:30.380
It was just very new to me.

441
00:22:30.820 --> 00:22:34.020
I usually end up in these dramas where I'm crying all day.

442
00:22:34.240 --> 00:22:37.780
So this is a much better way to spend your day.

443
00:22:38.020 --> 00:22:39.140
I remember that, too.

444
00:22:39.280 --> 00:22:44.540
And I remember my agents asking me if I wanted to do dramas in my downtime.

445
00:22:44.700 --> 00:22:46.740
You mentioned you were like, I've done a lot of dramas.

446
00:22:46.740 --> 00:22:47.560
I want to do a comedy.

447
00:22:47.660 --> 00:22:47.800
Yeah.

448
00:22:47.880 --> 00:22:49.800
Like, do you want to take a stab at some drama?

449
00:22:50.420 --> 00:22:52.600
And I was like, I don't think so.

450
00:22:52.900 --> 00:22:56.700
I think I'm really liking the jobs where I just laugh all day.

451
00:22:56.700 --> 00:22:58.960
Because I'm, like, in a good mood a lot.

452
00:22:59.220 --> 00:23:02.060
So let's just, let's keep doing the comedies.

453
00:23:02.800 --> 00:23:07.000
It was, it was, like, it was a happy place to work.

454
00:23:07.620 --> 00:23:10.740
My only request was if I could ever have a scene where I didn't frown.

455
00:23:12.120 --> 00:23:12.700
That's true.

456
00:23:12.700 --> 00:23:14.720
You had to frown your way through this comedy.

457
00:23:16.680 --> 00:23:20.600
Well, do you get recognized as Holly Flax, like, when you're out and about?

458
00:23:21.520 --> 00:23:22.520
Oh, I do.

459
00:23:22.740 --> 00:23:23.380
I do.

460
00:23:23.380 --> 00:23:31.300
And it's interesting, I'm sure you guys have noticed, there's being, being recognized when

461
00:23:31.300 --> 00:23:40.200
the show was on the air, and then the whole streaming version of the show that has kind

462
00:23:40.200 --> 00:23:41.740
of taken it to a whole new level.

463
00:23:41.960 --> 00:23:43.440
And everyone's getting younger.

464
00:23:43.880 --> 00:23:44.220
Yeah.

465
00:23:44.220 --> 00:23:46.560
It's like, my daughter's friends.

466
00:23:46.560 --> 00:23:53.100
And, um, um, so, yeah, I do, I still get, I still get recognized.

467
00:23:53.200 --> 00:23:59.080
You know, years ago, not that long ago, a few years ago, my family and our really good

468
00:23:59.080 --> 00:24:06.440
friends, we went to Scranton, because there's this amazing, um, antique store called All Good

469
00:24:06.440 --> 00:24:06.860
Things.

470
00:24:06.960 --> 00:24:13.980
And it's just a warehouse, a giant warehouse of just every old antique knob, hinge, dresser,

471
00:24:14.160 --> 00:24:15.200
door, toilet.

472
00:24:15.200 --> 00:24:16.060
Sign me up, Amy.

473
00:24:16.060 --> 00:24:17.360
It goes on, it goes on forever.

474
00:24:17.580 --> 00:24:18.220
It's really cool.

475
00:24:18.480 --> 00:24:23.380
And so we, we, we go there and then we're looking for something to eat and we're hungry

476
00:24:23.380 --> 00:24:29.040
and they pull in and I was kind of behind the eight ball on this and we, we pull into Coopers.

477
00:24:29.320 --> 00:24:29.940
Oh my gosh.

478
00:24:29.940 --> 00:24:32.720
And I go, I go, oh no, no, no, you guys, no.

479
00:24:32.920 --> 00:24:38.840
Cause I, I'm pretty, I mean, I don't get harassed being, you know, but I, I'm pretty shy.

480
00:24:39.060 --> 00:24:41.760
If someone comes up to me on the street, I say hello and thank you.

481
00:24:41.760 --> 00:24:44.240
But, but I also have a lot of privacy.

482
00:24:44.520 --> 00:24:49.820
People don't always recognize me, but I was like, hell no, I, we are not going into Coopers.

483
00:24:50.080 --> 00:24:51.740
I am Polly from the office.

484
00:24:51.940 --> 00:24:53.200
I cannot walk into Coopers.

485
00:24:53.320 --> 00:24:54.500
I cannot, I cannot, I cannot.

486
00:24:54.900 --> 00:24:56.980
And they're like, and my one friend, Anne was there.

487
00:24:57.040 --> 00:24:57.740
She's kind of naughty.

488
00:24:57.820 --> 00:24:59.660
She's like, oh, it'll be so good.

489
00:24:59.660 --> 00:25:01.040
We have to, we have to.

490
00:25:02.240 --> 00:25:07.580
So they talked me into it, but I go in and I've got my head down and I'm just like waiting.

